===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# On the [[day]] after the present day.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Works|volume=XV|chaptername=Third Branch—The Bill|page=63|passage=It was eight o'clock '''to-morrow''' evening when I buckled up my travelling writing-desk in its leather case, paid my Bill, and got on my warm coats and wrappers.}}
# At some point [[in the future]]; [[later on]]
#: {{ux|en|If you don’t get your life on track today, you’re going to be very sorry '''tomorrow'''.}}
# {{lb|en|possibly|obsolete}} On next (period of time other than a day, such as a week or a month), following the present (period of time).
#* '''1664''' March 28, debate in Great Britain's House of Commons, printed in '''1803''' in the ''Journals of the House of Commons'', page 538:
#*: ''Resolved, &c.'' That the House be Called over again on '''Tomorrow''' Month, being the Six-and-twentieth Day of ''April'' next.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1840|chapter=Melancholy Death of Amelia V|title=The Christian Guardian (and Church of England magazine)|page=60
|passage='You shall go to it on '''''to-morrow''' week'', so make haste and get well!'}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} On the next day (following some date in the past).
#* '''1717''' October 8, Robert Wodrow, in a letter to Mr. James Hart, printed in '''1828''', Robert Wodrow, ''The History of the Sufferings of the Church of Scotland'', page xxii:
#*: To prevent this, a committee for peace was proposed for '''to-morrow''', who heard the ministers and Mr. Anderson upon the heads of the affair, but in vain; when their complaint was given in in Synod, and referred to the next Synod {{...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1817|author=James Kirkton|title=The Secret and True History of the Church of Scotland|page=126
|passage={{...}} after he hade drunk liberally in the Advocate's house that same day, went to bed in health, but was taken up stark dead '''to-morrow''' morning; and such was the  testimony of honour heaven was pleased to allow Montrose&#39;s pompuous funerals.}}

